import VOA.Examples.TripletW2Nilpotent
import VOA.Core.ScreeningKernel

/-!
# `C₂`-cofiniteness of the triplet `W(p)`, at general `p`, by the screening route

The structural proof of Adamović–Milas, *On the triplet vertex algebra `W(p)`*,
Adv. Math. **217** (2008) 2664–2699 (arXiv:0707.1857), **Theorem 3.1**, formalized against
`Examples.TripletWp`'s `tripletWVOA p hp` — the subalgebra of the background-charge-deformed
lattice VOA `V_{L_p}` generated by `ω_λ, E, H, F`.

The whole point of this route (as opposed to Carqueville–Flohr's null-vector/character argument,
math-ph/0508015, which Adamović–Milas call "rather lengthy") is that **no singular vector is ever
computed**.  Everything is the *lattice product formula* plus the fact that the long screening
`Q = (e^α)₍₀₎` is a **derivation**.

## The proof in one line

`F₍ₙ₎F = 0` for `n ≥ −2p` (lattice), `Q³F = 0` (weight/charge), `Q` a derivation ⟹ the three
relations `Ē² = F̄² = 0`, `H̄² = −Ē F̄`, hence `H̄⁴ = 0` in `A₂ = W(p)/C₂(W(p))`; four commuting
nilpotent algebra generators then give `dim A₂ < ∞`.

## Main results

* `smul_latChargeGen_eq_zero` — `(e^α)₍ₙ₎e^β = 0` for `n ≥ −⟨α,β⟩`, from `Yeαvec_tmul` and
  `EEVec_vacuum`.  This is Adamović–Milas (2.4) in the only form the proof needs.
* `smul_Ftrip_Ftrip` — `F₍ₙ₎F = 0` for `n ≥ −2p`, its specialization at `β = α = −α_p`.
* `Qwp`, `Qwp_eq_Qscr`, `Qwp_leibniz` — the long screening `Q = (e^α)₍₀₎` **presented as the
  generic `Screening.screening`**, which is what makes `Core.ScreeningKernel.screening_leibniz`
  (the `ℓ = 0` Borcherds slice) apply: `Q(u₍ₙ₎v) = (Qu)₍ₙ₎v + u₍ₙ₎(Qv)`, no hypothesis on `Q`.
  `Qwp_Ftrip`, `Qwp_Htrip` are `QF = H`, `QH = E`; `Qwp_Etrip` is `Q³F = 0`
  (`Screen.Qscr_Escr`).
* `HF_add_FH`, `EF_add_HH_add_HH_add_FE`, `EF_add_FE_add_two_HH`, `EH_add_HE`,
  `smul_Etrip_Etrip` — `Q, Q², Q³, Q⁴` applied to `F₍ₙ₎F = 0`.  The last two are the remaining
  clauses of Adamović–Milas (2.2), `Q(H₍ₙ₎H) = 0` and `E₍ₙ₎E = 0`; the third is the displayed
  relation `E₍₋₁₎F + F₍₋₁₎E + 2H₍₋₁₎H = 0` of their Theorem 3.1.
* `genOmega, genE, genH, genF, genWp`, `smul_coe`, `val_range_genWp` — the four generators as
  states of `W(p)`, and the fact that products in `W(p)` are the ambient products, so every
  relation above transports.
* `mk₂_genE_sq`, `mk₂_genF_sq`, `mk₂_genH_sq`, `mk₂_genH_pow_four` —
  `Ē² = F̄² = 0`, `H̄² = −ĒF̄`, `H̄⁴ = 0` in `A₂(W(p))`.
* `c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA` — **`C2Cofinite (tripletWVOA p hp)`**, from the two carried
  hypotheses `AdjoinGenWp` and `OmegaNilpotentWp` below.  `finrank_a₂_tripletWVOA_le` is the
  quantitative form: `ω̄ ^ m = 0` gives `dim_ℂ W(p)/C₂(W(p)) ≤ 16·m`.
  `c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA_two` is the `p = 2` instance.
* `AdjoinGenWp`, `adjoinGenWp_of_strongGenWp`, `c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA_of_strongGen` — the
  generation hypothesis in the **strictly weaker** form the proof actually consumes.
* `mk₂_genOmega_pow_eq_zero_iff`, `OmegaPowMem`, `pMulPow_genOmega_succ`,
  `c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA_of_omegaPowMem` — the residual turned into a **concrete vector
  membership** `L₋₂^m|0⟩ ∈ C₂(W(p))`, which is the shape Adamović–Milas Corollary 4.6 delivers
  (at `m = 3p − 1`, giving `dim ≤ 48p − 16`).

## ⚠ The two carried hypotheses, stated in full

Nothing else is missing: given these two, `C2Cofinite (tripletWVOA p hp)` is a theorem of this
file, for every `p ≥ 1`.

⚠ The generation hypothesis is carried in **two** forms.  The one every theorem below actually
takes is `AdjoinGenWp p hp` — *"`A₂(W(p))` is generated as a commutative `ℂ`-algebra by
`ω̄, Ē, H̄, F̄`"*, a statement about the **quotient only**, which is Adamović–Milas Theorem 3.1
step 1 / Abe Prop. 2.2.  `StrongGenWp p hp` (below) is the spanning statement about `W(p)` itself;
it *implies* `AdjoinGenWp` (`adjoinGenWp_of_strongGenWp`) and the converse fails in general, so
taking `AdjoinGenWp` as the hypothesis is a strict improvement.  `StrongGenWp` is retained because
it is the citable statement (AM Prop. 2.3(ii)) and because `Examples.TripletWpAmbient` reduces it
further (to `SmulClosedWp`, one closure clause in the ambient lattice VOA).

* **`StrongGenWp p hp`** — `iterProdSpan (tripletWVOA p hp) (range (genWp p hp)) = ⊤`, i.e.
  `W(p)` is *spanned* by the iterated products `g^{i₁}₍₋m₁₎⋯g^{iₖ}₍₋mₖ₎|0⟩`, `mⱼ ≥ 1`,
  `g^{iⱼ} ∈ {ω_λ, E, H, F}`.  This is Adamović–Milas **Proposition 2.3(ii)**.
  ⚠ It is *not* the definition of `tripletW p hp`, which is `VOASubalgebra.generated` — closure
  under **all** products `u₍ₙ₎v`, `n ∈ ℤ`, of **arbitrary** members.  Weak generation does not
  imply strong generation (commuting a non-negative mode to the right produces modes of
  *products* of generators, not of generators), so the hypothesis has real content.  AM prove it
  by induction on the lattice charge, using the Feigin–Fuchs structure theory of `V_L` as a
  Virasoro module and the relations `F₍₋2np−1₎e^{−nα} = e^{−(n+1)α}` etc.
  `val_range_genWp` records that the generating family really does sit over `Wp.tripGens p`.
  (`Modules.FiniteGenerationProof.iterProdSpan_eq_top` is *not* applicable: its hypothesis
  `span S ⊔ O₂ = ⊤` is the strictly stronger statement that the four classes span `A₂` linearly,
  which already implies `C₂`-cofiniteness by `TripletNilp.c2Cofinite_of_span_sup_O₂`.)

* **`OmegaNilpotentWp p hp`** — `IsNilpotent (mk₂ (tripletWVOA p hp) (genOmega p hp))`, i.e.
  `ω̄` is nilpotent in `A₂(W(p))`.  Deliberately **exponent-free**, so no guessed exponent can
  make it vacuous.  See the next section: this is the step whose printed justification in AM is
  not valid, and it is exactly where the mathematical content sits.

## ⚠⚠ What Adamović–Milas actually prove for `ω̄`, and why the printed argument is a non-sequitur

Their Theorem 3.1 runs: strong generation ⟹ `A₂` is generated by `{Ē,F̄,H̄,ω̄}`; (2.2) ⟹
`Ē² = F̄² = 0`; `Q²(F₍₋₁₎F) = 0` ⟹ `H̄² = −ĒF̄` ⟹ `H̄⁴ = 0`.  Then, verbatim:

> "Moreover, the description of Zhu's algebra from [A-2003] implies that `H̄² = C_p ω̄^{2p−1}`,
> `(C_p ≠ 0)`.  Since `H̄⁴ = 0`, we conclude that `ω̄^{4p−2} = 0`."

**This inference is invalid, in the direction stated.**

1. What [A-2003] supplies is a relation in **Zhu's algebra** `A(V) = V/O(V)`.  It reappears as
   AM's own Theorem 6.1(ii): `[H]*[H] = C_p P([ω])` with
   `P(x) = (x − h_{p,1})∏_{i=1}^{p−1}(x − h_{i,1})²`, a **degree-`2p−1`, non-homogeneous**
   polynomial — not the monomial `x^{2p−1}`.
2. `A(V)` and `R_V = V/C₂(V)` are **different quotients**, and the only comparison map between
   them is the epimorphism `π : V/C₂(V) ↠ gr_• A(V)` — which AM themselves state, on the page
   before their Proposition 4.1.  It goes **from** `R_V` **to** `gr A(V)`.  A relation in `A(V)`
   therefore pulls back to nothing: it says only that `H̄² − C_p ω̄^{2p−1} ∈ ker π`, and `ker π`
   is not controlled (it is precisely the discrepancy that makes `A(V)` finite-dimensional for
   many non-`C₂`-cofinite `V`).  Their Proposition 4.1 records the inequality in exactly this
   one-way form: `dim(V/C₂(V)) ≥ dim A(V)` **when `V` is already `C₂`-cofinite**.
3. So Theorem 3.1's last step assumes the conclusion's shape in the quotient where it is *not*
   available.  The relation `H̄² = C_p ω̄^{2p−1}` in `R_{W(p)}` may well be true — it is the
   image of Theorem 6.1(ii) under `π`, read backwards — but it is not proved there.

**The repair is inside the same paper, and gives a better bound.**  AM's **Corollary 4.6**
proves `ω̄^{3p−1} = 0` in `W(p)/C₂(W(p))` by an argument that never crosses the two quotients in
the wrong direction: an identity **in `V_L` itself**,

  `Q²e^{−2α} = A·H₍₋2p−1₎H + (B_p·L(−2)^{3p−1} + w).𝟙`,  `A ≠ 0`, `B_p ≠ 0`,

with `w ∈ 𝒰(Vir_{≤−2})` a combination of monomials each containing some `L(−n)`, `n ≥ 3`.  Since
`H₍₋2p−1₎H ∈ C₂`, `Q²e^{−2α} = E₍₋2p−1₎F + F₍₋2p−1₎E + 2H₍₋2p−1₎H ∈ C₂` and `w.𝟙 ∈ C₂`, the
identity forces `B_p·ω̄^{3p−1} = 0`.  Corollary 4.6 is stated *after* Theorem 3.1 but does not
depend on it (its inputs are Lemmas 4.1–4.2 and the explicit lattice evaluation in the proof of
Theorem 4.3 — none of which uses `C₂`-cofiniteness), so the reordering is legitimate and there is
no circularity.  AM even remark that it "gives a better upper bound … compared to the one that
could be extracted from Theorem 3.1".  What it still imports from [A-2003]/[AdM] is
(a) `H₍₋2p−1₎H = C·Q²e^{−2α} + v'`, `C ≠ 0`, `v' ∈ 𝒰(Vir).𝟙` (their Lemma 4.2), and
(b) `A(M(1)‾_p) ≅ ℂ[x]`, used to read `B_p ≠ 0` off `Φ_p` — both genuine imports.

Formalizing `OmegaNilpotentWp` therefore means formalizing Corollary 4.6, i.e. the Virasoro-word
identity above, **not** the printed Theorem 3.1 step.  With `m = 3p−1` the bound of
`finrank_a₂_tripletWVOA_le` reads `dim W(p)/C₂(W(p)) ≤ 48p − 16`; with AM's own (unjustified)
`m = 4p−2` it would read `64p − 32`.  The expected true value is `6p − 1` (Abe's `dim ≤ 11` at
`p = 2`), so neither bound is sharp — the engine's `∏ mᵢ` is crude, as expected.

## Status

Every declaration in this file is proved, `sorry`-free, `admit`-free, `native_decide`-free, and
axiom-clean: `#print axioms` on `smul_latChargeGen_eq_zero`, `smul_Ftrip_Ftrip`,
`smul_Etrip_Etrip`, `EF_add_FE_add_two_HH`, `mk₂_genE_sq`, `mk₂_genF_sq`, `mk₂_genH_sq`,
`mk₂_genH_pow_four`, `val_range_genWp`, `adjoinGenWp_of_strongGenWp`, `c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA`,
`finrank_a₂_tripletWVOA_le` all return `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`.
The theorem `VOA.pbw` is **not** used.

`c2Cofinite_tripletWVOA` is **conditional on `AdjoinGenWp` and `OmegaNilpotentWp`**, which are
explicit `Prop` arguments; it is not unconditional and is not presented as such.

This file **is** imported by `VOA/Examples.lean` (next to
`Examples.TripletW2Nilpotent` and `Examples.TripletWp`), so the aggregate
`lake build` does cover it.

⚠ The library does **not** prove `E ≠ 0` or `W(p) ⊊ V_{L_p}` for general `p` (both hold at `p = 2`:
certificates `L18Checks.Etrip_two_ne_zero`, `L18Checks.tripletW_two_carrier_ne_top`,
`Notes/Textbook/certificates/L18_Checks.lean`, not library theorems), nor that
`tripletW p hp = ker Q̃` (AM Prop 2.3(i)), nor `W(2) ≅ SympFerm.tripletW2`.  Nothing here repairs or relies on any of those.  In particular the
`E`-relations `Ē² = 0` and `H̄² = −ĒF̄` are **not known to be non-degenerate**: were `E = 0` they
would collapse to `0 = 0` and `H̄² = 0`.  The conclusion is unaffected either way (`H̄⁴ = 0`
holds in both cases), and `ω`, `H`, `F` *are* proved non-zero (`Wp.omegaTrip_ne_zero`,
`Wp.Htrip_ne_zero`, `Wp.Ftrip_ne_zero`), as is `(tripletW p hp).carrier ≠ ⊥`, so
`C2Cofinite (tripletWVOA p hp)` is not a statement about the zero algebra.

⚠ Every statement below is quantified over `p` with `hp : 1 ≤ p`, matching `Examples.TripletWp`.
Adamović–Milas take `p ≥ 2`; at `p = 1` the triplet degenerates (`W(1) = L(1,0)`, and `E,H,F,ω`
generate all of `V_{L_1}`).  Nothing here breaks at `p = 1` — the relations are proved for
`n ≥ −2p` with `2p = 2` — but the *hypotheses* `StrongGenWp 1 _` / `OmegaNilpotentWp 1 _` are then
statements about a different algebra than the one AM discuss.

## References
* D. Adamović and A. Milas, *On the triplet vertex algebra `W(p)`*, Adv. Math. **217** (2008)
  2664–2699 (arXiv:0707.1857): Prop. 2.3, eq. (2.2), eq. (2.4), Thm 3.1, Lem. 4.1–4.2, Thm 4.3,
  Cor. 4.6, Thm 6.1.
* D. Adamović, *Classification of irreducible modules of certain subalgebras of free boson vertex
  algebra*, J. Algebra **270** (2003) 115–132: Lemma 3.1 (the original `E_iE = F_iF = 0`).
* T. Abe, *A ℤ₂-orbifold model of the symplectic fermionic VOA*, Math. Z. **255** (2007) 755–792
  (`p = 2`, `dim ≤ 11 = 6p−1`); Prop. 2.2 there is the "strong generation ⟹ `R_V` generated as a
  commutative algebra" step.
* N. Carqueville and M. Flohr, math-ph/0508015 — the alternative, longer route.  Not used.
-/

